---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { people, dead } from "../lib/people.js";
import stats from "../data/stats.json";
const here = people.filter((p) => [p.birthPlace, p.deathPlace, p.burialPlace]
  .some((x) => (x || "").startsWith("Piedimonte")));
const orphans = people.filter((p) => p.comp === 2);
---
<Base title="Piedimonte Etneo" eyebrow="Catania, Sicily"
      dek="The town on the north-east flank of Etna that gave this family its surname — and, of the four quarters, the one the archive knows least about.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{here.length}</div><div class="l">people placed at Piedimonte Etneo</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">1879</div><div class="l">the earliest Mazza birth with a date</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{stats.components[1]}</div><div class="l">Mazza here who are joined to nothing</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The town</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><em>Piedimonte Etneo</em> means the foot of the mountain, and it is exactly that: a comune of
      the province of Catania standing on the north-eastern slope of <strong>Etna</strong>, above
      Fiumefreddo and the Alcantara valley, looking out at the Ionian Sea. The neighbouring towns in
      this archive — <strong>Mascali</strong>, <strong>Giarre</strong>, <strong>Linguaglossa</strong>'s
      side of the mountain — are all within a few kilometres.</p>
      <p>Living on Etna is a recurring administrative fact, not a metaphor. In
      <strong>1928</strong> the eruption that destroyed the town of Mascali — the only town Etna has
      swallowed whole in modern times — happened eight years after <P n="Sebastiano Mazza" /> was
      born here, and the archive records Mascali as a birthplace for people in this family.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The Sicilian quarter is four towns, not one</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.2rem">Piedimonte Etneo gave the family its surname. It did
    not give it everybody.</p>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>For most of this archive's life the Sicilian side was a single town, and the family tree
      still says so: it puts <P n="Maria Nicotra" /> at Piedimonte Etneo, where her husband was born.
      <strong>Four consecutive documents, read in September 2026, take her and her father somewhere
      else</strong> — and the towns are all within eight kilometres of each other, down the same
      coast road.</p>
      <ul style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;line-height:1.75;color:var(--ink-2);padding-left:20px">
        <li><strong>Piedimonte Etneo</strong> — the <strong>Mazza</strong>, back to Mariano Mazza and
        Maria Catalano, both alive and working there in April 1871. Also where
        <strong>Venera Cassaniti</strong> was born in 1848, and where she and Giovanni Nicotra
        married on 7 October 1872.</li>
        <li><strong>Mascali</strong> — where Giovanni Nicotra was living in 1872, and where
        <strong>Maria Nicotra was born on 8 December 1875</strong>, at half past nine in the evening,
        in <em>contrada Sopra Pietrafitta</em>. Her birth act was found here after fourteen
        consecutive Piedimonte birth indexes had been read without her, because a wife went to her
        husband's comune and the children were registered there.</li>
        <li><strong>Giarre</strong> — where the 1872 publication certificate says Giovanni Nicotra
        himself was <em>nato</em>. <strong>Eleven Giarre birth years have now been read end to end,
        main runs and appendices, and they do not hold him</strong>; the only Nicotra there in that
        whole decade is a <em>Dottor Don</em> with a <em>Donna</em> wife, and Giovanni's father was a
        bracciante.</li>
        <li><strong>Riposto</strong> — the lower town, which has registered separately from Giarre
        since <strong>1821</strong>. It holds the ordinary Nicotra households that Giarre does not:
        two of them in 1847 alone. <strong>He is not found yet</strong>, and this is where the search
        now is.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:14px">None of that was in the family's export, and none of it changes who
      Michael Rocco Mazza's grandparents were. It changes where his great-grandmother was born, and
      it adds three comuni and about two thousand volumes to what this archive has to read.
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>What has been read →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The line that carries the name, and stops</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.2rem">Three men, and then nothing.</p>
    <div class="rec">
      <div class="k">Generation 3</div>
      <div class="v"><P n="Rosario Mazza" /> — no dates, no places, no record. He exists only because
      his son's record names him.</div><div class="w">—</div>
      <div class="k">Generation 2</div>
      <div class="v"><P n="Salvatore Mazza" /> — born at Piedimonte Etneo, died at Catania.
      Married <P n="Maria Nicotra" />.</div><div class="w">1879–1929</div>
      <div class="k">Generation 1</div>
      <div class="v"><P n="Sebastiano Mazza" /> — born here, arrived Fremantle 4 October 1949,
      died at Brisbane and buried at <a href={u("/nudgee/")}>Nudgee</a>.</div><div class="w">1920–2002</div>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">Four generations. It is the shortest of the
    <a href={u("/lines/")}>four quarters</a> by five generations, and the surname the whole archive is
    named after runs out at a man about whom precisely one fact is known: that he was Salvatore's
    father.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>The hundred and eleven</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.8rem">The family tree also contains
    <strong>{orphans.length} Mazza of Piedimonte Etneo</strong> — Mariano, Leonardo, Orazio, Umberto,
    Giuseppa, Serena, Graziella and the rest — who form a complete little world of their own:
    parents, children, marriages, cousins. <strong>Not one record joins them to Sebastiano's line.</strong></p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">They are almost certainly the same family. A town of a few
    thousand people does not produce two unrelated Mazza kindreds. But "almost certainly" is not a
    document, and the join has to be made by a record rather than by an assumption — most likely by
    the birth act of <strong>Salvatore Mazza, 1879</strong>, which would name his father Rosario and
    his father's age, or by <strong>Rosario's own marriage act</strong>. Until then this archive keeps
    them apart and says why. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The open questions →</a></p>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Placed at</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {orphans.slice(0, 200).map((p) => (
            <tr><td>{p.living ? p.name : <a href={u("/people/" + p.slug)}>{p.name}</a>}</td>
                <td>{p.birthPlace || "—"}</td>
                <td><span class="chip inf">unjoined</span></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
